Task: Define the landing colour tokens (Phase 1, home_page_rebuild_20260906)

WHAT CHANGED
Added a landing-surface palette to app/globals.css as CSS custom properties with
light and dark values, registered in @theme inline so Tailwind emits bg-landing-*,
text-landing-* and border-landing-* utilities.

FILES
  M app/globals.css  (+11 @theme entries, +11 tokens in :root, +11 in .dark)

WHY
NFR2. The page being replaced hardcodes hex literals throughout, which is why it is
effectively light-only today. Dark-theme support (NFR1) is not reachable without
tokens, so this is a prerequisite rather than a cleanup.

Light values are the prototype's own hex, converted to oklch to match this file's
existing convention (the rest of globals.css is oklch throughout):
  paper #fbf9f9 · warm #eee9df · slab/footer #030813
  brass #b89a5a · brass-text #775a19 · muted #45474c · hairline #c6c6cc

THE DARK PALETTE IS NOT AN INVERSION
The layout's rhythm comes from alternating bands. The 'Why it matters' slab earns its
drama in light theme by sitting 0.850 in lightness BELOW the paper around it. Inverting
the colours literally would sink an already-dark band into an already-dark page and
flatten the composition. The relationship inverts instead — the slab rises above its
neighbours rather than dropping below them:

  band           light L   dark L   delta vs base
  paper (base)     0.984    0.163   —
  warm             0.935    0.212   -0.049 -> +0.049
  slab             0.134    0.268   -0.850 -> +0.105
  footer           0.134    0.121   stays below base, anchoring the page bottom

Brass dims 0.699 -> 0.600 on the CTA so the band does not glare on a dark page.
brass-text lifts 0.485 -> 0.720 to survive as an eyebrow colour on dark ground.
Text on the brass CTA stays ink in both themes.

NAMING DEVIATION FROM THE PLAN
The plan called this token 'ink'. It ships as 'slab' because in dark theme the band is
lighter than the page around it, so a colour-derived name would actively mislead. The
token is named for its role. --landing-fg carries the text colour that 'ink' described.

SCOPING
These tokens add names rather than redefine existing ones, and nothing outside
components/landing/ will reference them, so they cannot alter dashboard surfaces.

